Check 6 (MaxBytesReader correctness):
Code order in handleFileUpload: `r.Body = http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body,
maxUploadBatchBytes)` runs BEFORE `r.ParseMultipartForm(10 << 20)`, so the cap
applies to everything ParseMultipartForm reads, including temp-file spillover.
Built a standalone repro (not committed to the target repo — written to
/tmp scratchpad, run via `go run`) that reproduces the exact wrap-then-parse
sequence used in handleFileUpload with a multipart body 1MB over the 50MB cap:

  ParseMultipartForm error (expected, non-panic): http: request body too large

No panic; ParseMultipartForm returns a plain error, which handleFileUpload's
existing `if err != nil { http.Error(w, "File too large",
http.StatusBadRequest); return }` already catches. The comment on the
ParseMultipartForm(10<<20) call is corrected in the diff to clarify it's only
the memory-spill threshold, not a request-size cap — matches the spec's
"previously-fictional 10MB comment becomes a real cap" language.
✓ PASS

---
KNOWN FLAGGED DISCREPANCY — ruling on the security property:

sanitizeUploadFilename (unchanged) is:
  filename = filepath.Base(strings.ReplaceAll(filename, `\`, "/"))
  if filename == "." || filename == "" || strings.Contains(filename, "..") {
      return "", os.ErrInvalid
  }

filepath.Base always returns the final path element with no separator
character remaining in the result — by construction, everything before the
last "/" is discarded, and backslashes are normalized to "/" first, so
mixed-separator inputs (e.g. "..\..\evil3.csv") are covered too. A string with
zero "/" characters cannot function as a multi-segment relative path, so
"filename" containing the two literal characters ".." (if it survives, e.g.
"..evil.csv") cannot resolve as a parent-directory reference when passed to
filepath.Join(cfg.DataDirectory, filename) — Join/Clean only interprets ".."
as a parent reference when it is a distinct path segment (bounded by
separators), and there are none left. The one case where the base name IS
exactly ".." (a bare ".." upload) is caught explicitly by the
strings.Contains(filename, "..") check before any join happens.

I independently verified this rather than taking the worker's word: read the
sanitizer, traced filepath.Base's guarantee (no separator survives in its
return value), and confirmed via
TestHandleFileUpload_BatchTraversalNamesDoNotEscapeOrAbort — which the worker
added specifically for this — that "../evil.csv", "/etc/evil2.csv", and
"..\..\evil3.csv" all land inside dataDir at their base name and are absent
from dataDir's parent, and a bare ".." is rejected outright. This is a
positive (constructive) proof from the sanitizer's own logic, not merely
"the test says so."

RULING: the security property — no write escapes DataDirectory — is upheld by
the code as written. This is unrelated to whether the spec's test-#3 wording
("rejected") was honored verbatim; that wording question is explicitly a lead
decision per the task brief, not scored here.

