lead-only files called out in the task brief. Build/vet/test/staticcheck are
all clean, with the explorer package's new tests re-run forced (non-cached,
verbose) to confirm real execution. The three required tests genuinely assert
on-disk state, not just HTTP status codes, and would fail if the batch loop
silently dropped files after the first. Every pre-existing test that changed
did so because the spec's contract genuinely flipped (overwrite→skip,
request-abort→per-file-outcome), and each new assertion is as strong or
stronger than what it replaced — no weakening found. sanitizeUploadFilename
and handleFileDelete are byte-for-byte untouched. MaxBytesReader is wrapped
before ParseMultipartForm and independently confirmed (via an out-of-repo
repro of the same code sequence) to produce a clean, non-panicking error when
the cap is exceeded. The flagged security property (no write escapes
DataDirectory) is upheld by the code as written, verified independently.
